# The generator desugarer understood only the `.lock` SPELLING of "non-owned".
#
# A generator factory's receiver is written `{b: this.private.<marker>}` so the
# synthesised iterator class can read the private fields. The desugarer's three
# recognisers tested the leaf against the literal string "lock", so spelling it
# `.borrow` -- the same thing, once `.lock` retires -- meant the parameter was
# not recognised as a receiver at all.
#
# It failed SILENTLY. The generator still compiled and still built a class; the
# loop variable simply came back as the ITERATOR type rather than the yielded
# one, and the only symptom was a type error wherever the value was finally
# used. That is why this fixture RUNS: a compile alone proves nothing here.
#
# The emitter's other half of the same unblocking -- a wrapper create whose
# protocol argument is a bare name rather than a `.lock`-suffixed path -- is
# covered by `examples/io_buffered.z` and its siblings, which migrated to the
# bare form in the same commit and carry run goldens.
Bag: class {
    a: i64
    b: i64
    count: i64
} as {
    public: unit { :count :walk }
    walk: function {b: this.private.borrow} out (Iterator gives: i64) is {
        yield b.a
        yield b.b
    }
}

main: function is {
    g: Bag a: 10 b: 20 count: 2
    print "count=\{g.count}"
    # `x` must be the YIELDED type, not the iterator
    with it: (Bag.walk b: g) do for x: it loop print "walk: \{x}"
}
